1. e4 { [%eval +0.29] } 1... e6 { [%eval +0.38] } 2. d4 { [%eval +0.37] } 2... d5 { [%eval +0.32] } 3. Nc3 { [%eval +0.37] } 3... Bb4 { [%eval +0.59] } 4. Ne2 { [%eval +0.22] } 4... dxe4 { [%eval +0.13] } 5. a3 { [%eval +0.14] } 5... Be7 { [%eval +0.40] } 6. g3 { [%eval -0.64] } 6... f5 { [%eval -0.68] } 7. Bg2 { [%eval -0.35] } 7... c5 $2 { This move seemed a good idea at the time but really I should have played Nf6 and castled before attempting to grab some more central control. After all, I was playing the Black pieces and should have been looking more to consolidating and not losing ground, rather than getting too adventurous too soon. I paid for it later - I lost because my king was stuck in the middle. Still, lessons learned the hard way certainly stick in the mind and since this game I have always been mindful of the advisability of tucking the king away as soon as is convenient, and certainly before embarking upon any premature advances - Short, "Nigel Short's Chess Skills" [%eval -0.14] } 8. Bf4 { [%eval -0.55] } 8... Nc6 { [%eval +0.22] } 9. d5 { [%eval +0.26] } 9... Nd4 { [%eval +1.05] } 10. Nxd4 { [%eval +1.04] } 10... cxd4 { [%eval +1.06] } 11. Qxd4 { [%eval +1.16] } 11... Bf6 { [%eval +1.00] } 12. Qc4 { [%eval +1.04] } 12... Bxc3+ { [%eval +1.10] } 13. Qxc3 { [%eval +1.29] } 13... Nf6 { [%eval +1.27] } 14. dxe6 { [%eval +0.31] } 14... Bxe6 { [%eval +0.24] } 15. Rd1 { [%eval +0.21] } 15... Qe7 { [%eval +0.54] } 16. Bd6 { [%eval +0.64] } 16... Qf7 { [%eval +2.66] } 17. O-O { [%eval +2.45] } 17... Rc8 { [%eval +2.48] } 18. Qd4 { [%eval +1.64] } 18... b6 { [%eval +2.22] } 19. f3 { [%eval +2.90] } 19... Rc4 { [%eval +4.42] } 20. Qd2 { [%eval +2.80] } 20... Qb7 { [%eval +5.36] } 21. b3 { [%eval +5.38] } 21... Rc8 { [%eval +5.30] } 22. fxe4 { [%eval +5.96] } 22... Nxe4 { [%eval +6.11] } 23. Bxe4 { [%eval +6.13] } 23... fxe4 { [%eval +7.15] } 24. Bb4 { [%eval +7.25] } 24... Bh3 { [%eval +12.73] } 25. Qd6 { Source: 'Nigel Short: Chess Prodigy' by David Short (F