1. e4 { [%eval +0.34] } 1... e6 { [%eval +0.49] } 2. d4 { [%eval +0.39] } 2... d5 { [%eval +0.44] } 3. Nc3 { [%eval +0.39] } 3... Bb4 { [%eval +0.54] } 4. e5 { [%eval +0.61] } 4... Ne7 { [%eval +0.61] } 5. a3 { [%eval +0.66] } 5... Bxc3+ { [%eval +0.64] } 6. bxc3 { [%eval +0.66] } 6... c5 { [%eval +0.61] } 7. a4 { [%eval +0.47] } 7... Qa5 { [%eval +0.35] } 8. Bd2 { At the time the preferred move was Qd2 as played by Bobby Fischer vs Weinstein in USA Championship. I didn't like this move because it allowed Black to swap Queens by cxd4 which didn't suit my style so I started playing Bd2 which, over time, became the standard move in this position. [%eval +0.33] } 8... Nbc6 { [%eval +0.50] } 9. Nf3 { [%eval +0.49] } 9... Bd7 { [%eval +0.49] } 10. Be2 { [%eval +0.45] } 10... f6 { [%eval +0.52] } 11. O-O { [%eval +0.35] } 11... O-O-O { [%eval +1.03] } 12. Qb1 { Nowadays I'd prefer to play c4 once Black has 0-0-0 as the game opens up for the 2 Bishops which suits my style [%eval +0.29] } 12... c4 { [%eval +0.28] } 13. Qb2 { [%eval +0.11] } 13... Qc7 { [%eval +0.04] } 14. Rfb1 { [%eval +0.14] } ( 14. a5 $5 Nxa5 15. Rfb1 { By sacrificing the "a" pawn this ensures that the Rook on the "a" file is not shut out of play } ) 14... Na5 $1 { Making it virtually impossible to attack Black's King. This was the first time I'd seen this move and it was extremely worrying that my attacking chances were effectively reduced to zero so early on and, to add salt to the wound, by a junior [%eval +0.80] } 15. Qa3 { [%eval +0.55] } 15... Ng6 { [%eval +0.46] } 16. Be3 { My best chance is to sacrifice the "e" pawn and hope that my Be3 can create some attacking chances against the Black King [%eval -0.05] } 16... Kb8 { [%eval +0.29] } 17. Bf1 { Aiming to bring the Bishop to g2 or h3 to become more active [%eval +0.43] } 17... Ka8 { It's not for me to criticise Nigel's defensive moves, which show a degree of maturity for such a young player, but with the King in the corner it is more likely to be attacked by my Bishop when it gets to g2 although Black's Bishop can come to c6 to defend the King [%eval +0.40] } 18. Rb2 { [%eval +0.12] } 18... b6 { [%eval +0.07] } 19. Rab1 { [%eval -0.33] } 19... Rb8 { [%eval +0.23] } 20. g3 { [%eval +0.23] } 20... Nc6 { [%eval +0.12] } 21. Bg2 { [%eval +0.10] } 21... Rhf8 { [%eval +0.23] } 22. Nd2 $1 { With possible threats of sacrificing on c4 and playing a5 at the right time. [%eval +0.01] } 22... fxe5 { [%eval -0.12] } 23. dxe5 { [%eval -0.25] } 23... Ngxe5 $6 { Taking the "e" pawn gives White's Bishop on e3 much more scope. I think he shouldn't have taken it yet [%eval -0.11] } 24. Bf4 { [%eval -0.94] } 24... h6 { [%eval -0.51] } 25. a5 $1 { Sacrificing the "a" pawn to open up the file for the Queen and to temporarily take support from the other Knight [%eval -1.11] } 25... Nxa5 { [%eval -0.86] } 26. Re1 { [%eval -1.24] } 26... Nac6 { [%eval -1.26] } 27. Ra2 { [%eval -1.25] } ( 27. Nxc4 $2 Nxc4 $19 ) 27... Rxf4 { [%eval -1.34] } ( 27... a5 28. Nxc4 $1 Rxf4 29. Nxe5 Rxf2 30. Nxd7 Rxg2+ 31. Kxg2 Qxd7 $13 ) 28. gxf4 { [%eval -0.94] } 28... Ng4 { [%eval -1.16] } 29. h3 { [%eval -1.19] } 29... Qxf4 { [%eval +0.13] } 30. Nf3 { [%eval +0.09] } 30... Nf6 { [%eval +0.01] } 31. Ne5 { [%eval +0.01] } 31... a5 { [%eval +2.32] } 32. Nxd7 { [%eval +2.13] } 32... Nxd7 { [%eval +1.90] } 33. Rxe6 { [%eval +1.93] } 33... Rf8 { [%eval +1.86] } 34. Ra1 $1 { [%eval +0.89] } ( 34. Qd6 Qxf2+ 35. Kh1 Ndb8 36. Qxd5 Rf6 37. Re7 Qf5 38. Qxc4 $16 ) 34... Nde5 { [%eval +2.83] } 35. Rxe5 $1 { [%eval +4.05] } 35... Nxe5 { [%eval +4.60] } 36. Bxd5+ { [%eval +5.41] } 36... Kb8 { [%eval +5.76] } 37. Qd6+ { [%eval +5.79] } 37... Kc8 { [%eval +5.45] } 38. Be6+ { I knew I had him in a mating net and threw this move in to gain time on the clock. [%eval +5.65] } ( 38. Qxb6 Rf6 39. Be6+ Rxe6 40. Qxe6+ Kc7 41. Qe7+ Kc6 42. Rxa5 Nf3+ 43. Kf1 Nd2+ 44. Kg2 Qf3+ 45. Kg1 Qd1+ 46. Kh2 Nf3+ 47. Kg3 $18 ) 38... Kb7 { [%eval +5.75] } 39. Bd5+ { [%eval +5.74] } 39... Ka6 $2 { Perhaps Nigel didn't like the previous variation starting with Qxb6 or perhaps he didn't see the mate at the time...knowing Nigel he would be in time trouble and was looking for any way to win the game [%eval #3] } ( 39... Ka7 40. Qe7+ Ka6 41. Bb7+ Kb5 42. Rb1+ Ka4 43. Rb4+ Ka3 44. Rb3+ Ka2 45. Qa3# ) 40. Rxa5+ $3 { Perhaps he had seen the mate (40...Kxa5 41.Qa3+ Kb5 42.Qb4+ Ka5 43.Qa4 mate) as he resigned immediately  Source: "Forever a 180" by Oliver Jackson (self-published) via Nigel Short [%eval #2] } 1-0

