1. e4 { [%eval +0.31] } 1... c5 { [%eval +0.34] } 2. Nf3 { [%eval +0.38] } 2... d6 { [%eval +0.51] } 3. Bb5+ { [%eval +0.21] } 3... Bd7 { [%eval +0.15] } 4. Bxd7+ { [%eval +0.23] } 4... Qxd7 { [%eval +0.28] } 5. O-O { [%eval +0.19] } ( 5. c4 Nc6 6. d4 cxd4 7. Nxd4 g6 8. Nc3 Bg7 9. Be3 Nf6 10. h3 O-O 11. O-O Rac8 12. b3 Rfd8 13. Rc1 e6 14. f4 d5 15. cxd5 exd5 16. e5 Ne8 17. Na4 Qe7 $2 ( 17... b6 18. Qd2 Nxd4 19. Qxd4 Rxc1 20. Rxc1 Nc7 21. Rd1 Ne6 22. Qd2 d4 23. Bf2 Bh6 24. g3 Nxf4 $5 25. gxf4 Qxh3 26. Nb2 Qg4+ 27. Kf1 Bxf4 { With good chances } ) 18. Qd2 Nxd4 19. Qxd4 b6 20. Rxc8 Rxc8 21. Qxd5 Rd8 22. Qc4 Qd7 23. Nc3 Qd3 24. Qxd3 Rxd3 25. Nd1 Nc7 26. Kf2 f6 27. Ke2 Rd7 28. exf6 Bxf6 29. Nf2 Re7 30. Rd1 Nb5 31. Kf3 Kf7 32. Rd3 Nc3 33. a4 Ke6 34. Ne4 Nxe4 35. Kxe4 Rc7 36. g4 Rg7 37. f5+ gxf5+ 38. gxf5+ Ke7 39. Bf4 Rg2 40. Bd6+ Kf7 41. Bb8 Re2+ 42. Kd5 Re7 43. Kc6 Be5 44. Bxe5 Rxe5 45. Rd7+ Kf6 46. Rxa7 h5 47. Kxb6 Re3 48. b4 Rxh3 49. a5 Kxf5 50. a6 Ra3 51. Rh7 { 1-0 (51) Short,N (2050)-Jackson,O (2220) Manchester 1978 } ) 5... Nc6 { [%eval +0.23] } 6. c3 { [%eval +0.23] } 6... Nf6 { [%eval +0.40] } 7. Re1 { [%eval +0.23] } 7... g5 $5 { This is a typical pawn sacrifice which gives good attacking chances. Ideal against this young "kid" who probably wasn't that good at defending [%eval +0.62] } 8. Nxg5 { [%eval +0.49] } 8... Rg8 { [%eval +0.84] } 9. f4 $6 { Presumably White wants to be able to guard g2 laterally with Qe2 but this is weakening [%eval -0.32] } ( 9. d4 $1 h6 10. Nf3 Qg4 11. g3 cxd4 12. cxd4 Nxe4 $4 13. d5 $18 ) 9... h6 { [%eval -0.30] } 10. Nf3 { [%eval -0.27] } 10... Qg4 { [%eval -0.23] } 11. Qe2 { [%eval -0.14] } ( 11. g3 Qxf4 $11 ) 11... Nxe4 { [%eval -0.20] } 12. d3 { [%eval -0.54] } 12... Nf6 { [%eval -0.46] } 13. Nfd2 { [%eval -0.97] } 13... Qg6 { [%eval -0.27] } 14. Ne4 { [%eval -0.30] } 14... O-O-O { [%eval -0.19] } 15. a3 { [%eval -2.19] } 15... e5 { [%eval -0.18] } 16. b4 { [%eval -2.01] } 16... exf4 { [%eval -1.83] } 17. bxc5 { [%eval -4.07] } 17... d5 { [%eval -2.90] } 18. Nxf6 { [%eval -2.18] } 18... Qxf6 { [%eval -2.34] } 19. Qf2 { [%eval -2.85] } 19... f3 { [%eval -2.74] } 20. g3 { [%eval -3.34] } 20... Ne5 { [%eval -2.48] } 21. Re3 { [%eval -4.30] } 21... d4 { [%eval -3.67] } 22. cxd4 { [%eval -3.78] } 22... Rxd4 { [%eval -3.89] } 23. Nd2 { [%eval -3.89] } 23... Rxd3 { [%eval -4.07] } 24. Ne4 $2 { Missing the tactics based on the loose Rook on a1 [%eval -5.84] } ( 24. Bb2 Qg5 25. Rxe5 Qxd2 26. Qxd2 Rxd2 27. Bc3 Rd3 28. Bb4 $17 ) 24... Rd1+ { [%eval -6.04] } 25. Re1 { [%eval -6.24] } 25... Rxe1+ { [%eval -6.22] } 26. Qxe1 { [%eval -6.16] } 26... f2+ { [%eval -6.28] } 27. Qxf2 { [%eval -6.15] } 27... Nf3+ { [%eval -6.39] } 28. Kg2 { [%eval -6.29] } 28... Qxa1 $2 { Not thinking that White may have some chances - i expected him to resign as I'm a Rook up and we've both got pieces en prise. If I'd looked a little closer I would have played the following line which wins trivially [%eval -4.16] } ( 28... Nh4+ $1 29. Kf1 ( 29. Kh3 $4 Qe6+ $19 ) 29... Qxa1 { Now we've still got a piece each en prise but I'm taking with CHECK so there's no time for White to create counterplay and he resigns immediately a Rook down } 30. Qe3 Nf5 31. Qf4 Nd4 32. Qe3 Nb3 33. Qxb3 Qxc1+ $19 ) 29. Qxf3 { [%eval -4.21] } 29... Qxc1 { I planned to give back my Bishop and win with Rook against Knight [%eval -2.22] } 30. Qf5+ { [%eval -2.15] } 30... Kd8 { [%eval -2.32] } 31. Qd5+ { [%eval -2.37] } 31... Kc8 { [%eval -2.58] } ( 31... Ke8 32. Nf6+ Ke7 33. Qd6# ) 32. Qf5+ { [%eval -2.53] } 32... Kb8 { [%eval -2.26] } 33. Qe5+ { [%eval -2.07] } 33... Bd6 { [%eval -2.68] } 34. Nxd6 { [%eval -3.04] } ( 34. cxd6 Rd8 35. Qe7 Qc2+ 36. Kf3 Qc8 37. Nc5 Qc6+ 38. Ne4 Qd7 $19 ) 34... Qd2+ { [%eval -2.68] } 35. Kh3 { [%eval -2.78] } 35... Qg5 $4 { [%eval +6.89] } ( 35... Ka8 36. Qe4 Rb8 37. a4 h5 38. a5 a6 39. Qf3 h4 40. gxh4 Qd4 41. Qxf7 Qxc5 42. Ne4 Qxa5 $19 ) 36. Qe4 { I completely overlooked this move which wins outright. I was utterley distraught that I'd lost the game TWICE. Firstly when winning a Rook but overlooking a zwishenzug and secondly by blundering into a mate when still in a winning position [%eval +6.80] } 36... Kc7 { [%eval +9.55] } 37. Qxb7+ { Source: "Forever a 180" by Oliver Jackson (self-published) via Nigel Short. (n.b. Oliver Jackson has down as round 4, 28 August 1977, but I think it is likely to be from the Chorley Invitation (25-31 August 1979) reported briefly in CHESS, September 1979, Vol.44/823-4, p336: 1 J Horner 7½/9; 2-4 V Knox, J Littlewood, N Short 6½, no other scores given) [%eval +9.31] } 1-0

